<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-img"><img src="/assets/img/photos/desk-calculator.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/desk-calculator-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/desk-calculator.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="900" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Appraisal terms</li></ol></nav><p class="eyebrow on-dark">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Terms, Defined in Plain English</h1><p class="lede">40 words you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards. Type in the box to filter.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><div class="search"><label for="term-search">Search the terms</label><input id="term-search" type="search" placeholder="Try “holdback” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><output id="term-count" for="term-search">40 terms</output></div><dl class="terms"><div><dt id="t-0">Actual cash value</dt><dd>The value of damaged property at the time of loss, usually replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay this first and the rest after repairs.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-1">Actual cash value holdback</dt><dd>The portion of a replacement cost claim, usually recoverable depreciation, that an insurer withholds until repairs or replacement are completed.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-2">Additional living expenses</dt><dd>Extra costs of living elsewhere, such as rent, hotel bills and added mileage, while a covered loss makes your home unlivable. Often called Coverage D or loss of use.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-3">Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of the damage under the policy’s valuation terms. This is what appraisal decides.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-4">Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision that sets the amount of loss, signed by any two of the two appraisers and the umpire.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-5">Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy provision that lets either party demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss and describes how the process works.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-6">Appraisal demand</dt><dd>The written notice one party sends the other to invoke the appraisal clause.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-7">Contents inventory</dt><dd>A room-by-room list of damaged or destroyed personal property with descriptions, ages, quantities and replacement prices.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-8">Coverage A, B, C and D</dt><dd>Common homeowners policy sections: A is the dwelling, B is other structures, C is personal property and D is loss of use or additional living expenses.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-9">Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy that lists the named insured, property address, coverage limits, deductibles and policy period.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-10">Deductible</dt><dd>The part of a covered loss you pay before the insurer pays. It may be a flat dollar amount or a percentage of a coverage limit.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-11">Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value for age, wear and condition that separates actual cash value from replacement cost.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-12">Drip edge</dt><dd>Metal flashing installed along roof edges to direct water into the gutters and away from the fascia. Whether it is required can depend on local code.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-13">Earth movement exclusion</dt><dd>Policy language that excludes damage from earthquakes, landslides, sinkholes, subsidence and similar ground movement unless an endorsement adds coverage.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-14">Holdback</dt><dd>Money the insurer withholds from a payment, most often recoverable depreciation, until a condition such as completed repairs is met.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-15">Ice and water shield</dt><dd>A self-adhering waterproof underlayment applied at eaves, valleys and penetrations to protect against ice dams and wind-driven rain.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-16">Kentucky FAIR Plan</dt><dd>The property insurer of last resort for Kentucky owners who cannot find coverage in the regular market.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-17">Line item</dt><dd>A single priced entry in an estimate, such as a quantity of shingles, a labor task or a fee.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-18">Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether replaced materials must reasonably match undamaged materials, which depends on policy language and the facts.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-19">Mine subsidence</dt><dd>Ground sinking caused by the collapse of underground mine workings, which standard policies usually exclude unless separate coverage applies.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-20">Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping a roof or drying out a wet room. Most policies require it.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-21">Mortgagee clause</dt><dd>The policy provision that protects the lender’s interest, which is why mortgage companies are often named on claim checks.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-22">Named insured</dt><dd>The person or entity listed on the declarations page as the policyholder.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-23">Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs to repair or rebuild to current building codes, usually subject to a separate limit.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-24">Overhead and profit</dt><dd>A contractor’s charge for running the business and earning a margin, commonly added when a job needs a general contractor to coordinate several trades.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-25">Party appraiser</dt><dd>The appraiser each side selects in an appraisal. Most policies require this person to be competent and impartial.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-26">Percentage deductible</dt><dd>A deductible equal to a percentage of a coverage limit, often the dwelling limit, commonly used for wind or hail losses.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-27">Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed that some policies require within a set time after the insurer asks for it.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-28">Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation the insurer will pay back once you complete repairs or replacement under a replacement cost policy.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-29">Replacement cost value</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without deducting depreciation.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-30">Reservation of rights</dt><dd>A letter in which the insurer continues handling a claim while saying it may later deny coverage for some or all of it.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-31">Scope of loss</dt><dd>The description of what is damaged and what work is needed to repair it, before pricing is applied.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-32">Square (roofing)</dt><dd>A unit of roof area equal to 100 square feet.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-33">Steep charge</dt><dd>An added labor charge for working on roofs above a certain pitch, where extra safety measures slow the work.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-34">Subrogation</dt><dd>The insurer’s right, after paying a claim, to recover its payment from a third party who caused the loss.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-35">Suit limitation</dt><dd>The policy clause that sets how long you have to file a lawsuit against the insurer. Kentucky law limits how short it can be.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-36">Supplement</dt><dd>A request for additional payment for damage or costs discovered after the original estimate, often during repairs.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-37">Tear-off</dt><dd>Removing existing roofing layers down to the deck before installing a new roof.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-38">Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral chosen by the appraisers, or appointed by a court, to decide items the appraisers cannot agree on.</dd></div><div><dt id="t-39">Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers and contractors that prices line items using regional price lists.</dd></div></dl></article>
